    "description": "The Billing Workshop is a transaction-script application used by warehouse staff and scheduled billing jobs. CustomerRepository contains a query that loads customers, BillingJob repeats a customer lookup with different filtering, and CustomerMaintenance embeds inserts and updates. Each location independently handles parameter binding, result mapping, and persistence errors. Customer records are passed between scripts as arrays of values, so a script can reorder columns without an immediate failure. Create one CustomerRow type that mirrors the customer table and exposes the row's read, save, update, and remove operations. Scripts should obtain rows through a shared customer-table component and retain the row behavior during a transaction. The database mapping must remain explicit because the schema is owned by another system and cannot be reshaped into a richer business model. Existing reports may continue returning tabular records, but the row type is the only object permitted to contain persistence details for individual customers.",
    "decision_statement": "Should each database row be represented by an object that owns its own read and write operations, rather than repeating row mapping and SQL in transaction scripts?",
    "notes": "row-data-gateway fits the CustomerRow type owning per-row read and write operations; table-data-gateway and data-access-object are the table-level and store-abstraction alternatives, with dao-factory supporting creation."
  },
